                <pb facs="0204" n="194" sameAs="frus:frus1941v06/pg194"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.85/370: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Peru</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Norweb</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-20T14:00:00-05:00">April 20, 1941—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>73. From the Under Secretary. Your 131, April 18, 5 p.m. I am sure you will
                    realize from reading the suggested preliminary text of the resolution that there
                    is nothing whatever in the text of the resolution which would support Dr. Solf’s
                    thesis that the resolution itself indicates or determines the way in which the
                    vessels would be used. I think it would be desirable for you to make clear
                    immediately that insofar as the text of the resolution is concerned, it leaves
                    to every one of the American Governments full liberty of action as to the use of
                    the vessels and that, consequently, any government which, either for reasons of
                    policy or because of its domestic legislation, would desire to use such ships as
                    it might take over solely in inter-American trade is at complete liberty to do
                    so.</p>
                <pb facs="0205" n="195" xml:id="pg195"/>
                <p>Please endeavor to clarify this important point as quickly as possible and
                    telegraph me the results of any further conversations you may have in this
                    regard. [Welles.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
